comments.body:
The way that cancellation works is that if a command is already in progress, it has to drop the connection (there's no other way).

The convention for doing cancellation should probably look a bit like this from your perspective:

```csharp
try {
    client.DoSomethingAsync (..., cancellationToken);
} catch (OperationCanceledException) {
    if (!client.IsConnected) {
        Reconnect ();
    }
}
```

I should note that the `ImapEngine` always checks cancelation state *before* issuing the command as well, so it's possible that the connection is not dropped if the command hasn't been sent yet, so you should always check `client.IsConnected`.

What you can do is request specific header fields (such as Received) like this:

```csharp
var fields = new HashSet<string> ();
fields.Add ("Received");

inbox.Fetch (uids, MessageSummaryItems.UniqueId | MessageSummaryItems.Envelope, fields);
```

At this point you can access the `Headers` property of each message summary that is returned.

This is probably the easiest solution:

```csharp
var message = new MimeMessage ();
var builder = new BodyBuilder ();

builder.Attachments.Add (attachmentName, attachment, ContentType.Parse (attachmentType));

message.Body = builder.ToMessageBody ();
```

This assumes that `attachmentName` and `attachmentType` are strings while `attachment` is a `byte[]`.

